Background. The Weihrauch degrees form a lattice obtained from the preordering of (partial multi-valued) functions on represented spaces under Weihrauch reducibility ≤_W. On this structure one considers several natural operators:
 • the binary join ⊔ and meet ⊓ (lattice supremum and infimum with respect to ≤_W);
 • the parallel product ×, taking (f,g) to the function that solves an instance of f and an instance of g in parallel;
 • the finite-parallelization (or "star") operator ∗, where f∗ solves finitely many instances of f in parallel;
 • the compositional product ⋆, where f ⋆ g is the hardest problem Weihrauch-reducible to "first apply g, then apply f";
 • the diamond operator ⋄, capturing finitely-iterated compositions;
 • the implication / residual → (right adjoint to ⋆);
 • the identity degree 1;
 • the (parallelization) jump operator ′, which assigns to a Weihrauch degree f the degree f′ defined by composing f with the limit operator on Baire space.

It is known that ⊔ and ⊓ are first-order definable from ≤_W; that ∗ is definable from 1, ≤_W and ×; that ⋄ is definable from 1, ≤_W and ⋆; and that → is definable from ⋆ and ≤_W.

Question. Is the jump operator ′ on the Weihrauch degrees first-order definable from the remaining structure (≤_W together with 1, ×, ⋆, ⊔, ⊓, ∗, ⋄, →)? That is, does there exist a first-order formula φ(x,y) in this signature such that for all Weihrauch degrees f and g, φ(f,g) holds if and only if g = f′?

